#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "6edc9a54-82e2-5a3b-a7b5-379328a78c99" :question/math-academy-id "e-21818" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of differential equations are linear?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}^{2}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}(t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms $x_1^2(t)$ and $\\sin(x_2(t)).$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=8t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=6, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the second system is linear."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "62d5bd71-8137-553b-b564-80e9c25b1f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-21819" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(3{e}^{5t})}^{′} \\\\ {(5{e}^{-3t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}15{e}^{5t} \\\\ -15{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for $\\mathbf{x}_2(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t+5)}^{′} \\\\ {({t}^{2}-3)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t+5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}5t+25 \\\\ -3{t}^{2}+9\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\mathbf{x}_3(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(5{e}^{t})}^{′} \\\\ {(-3{e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}25{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128828 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dfdbdba-247c-5ddd-bb6e-5d9637140264" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Linear System of Differential Equations in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1cd2e15e-ef47-5244-91d1-85864a62d58d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21820" :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables). \nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128829 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "be14a013-2690-5fb8-8e2b-bbda1dca88b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21821" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{t}^{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=6, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\not\\equiv 0$ and $g \\not\\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={t}^{3} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f \\equiv 0$ and $g \\equiv 0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=5, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-2, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only the first and third systems are homogeneous."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d63e655-94cc-5cdc-9750-835757cf0980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126939} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ba9e86-0cfb-550b-8d05-9d7f31c6afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126940} {:answer/id #uuid "d503914a-3f4a-5640-a2a4-8de616210196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126941} {:answer/id #uuid "026a5fec-0704-5b86-81f3-969ad5e8b0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126942} {:answer/id #uuid "489fcca7-6e49-578f-8e8a-ab91d7d77011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ba9e86-0cfb-550b-8d05-9d7f31c6afdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126940} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a1507e8-f2c9-5125-98cf-d4c244f37154" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e0e3f4-a680-55b4-bb60-e56352a4c35c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339353" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ (5)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{5t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{4t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{5t} \\\\ 4{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(t-4)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ t-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2} \\\\ 4t-16\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18138e69-66ce-5601-a112-de4b10277292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303990} {:answer/id #uuid "de3a0a1f-a3a6-5d3e-8688-c60a78c3069b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303991} {:answer/id #uuid "bb653d60-d2dd-55b0-a674-e7745548f369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303992} {:answer/id #uuid "90b35e4c-4eb7-5be9-886d-df623d4accec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303993} {:answer/id #uuid "10451d41-e90a-505b-8295-b2abc9db3312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3a0a1f-a3a6-5d3e-8688-c60a78c3069b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303991} :answer-field/id #uuid "2df613fe-b769-5630-bd66-7a59f9395488" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c82bd721-0ced-5628-bfd8-e025b1cd1a13" :question/math-academy-id "q-339358" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (5t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(2{t}^{2})}^{′} \\\\ {(2t-8)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t-8\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{2t})}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{3}(t)$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c38a973b-7ad2-50cf-ad58-19a6d1d52d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303997} {:answer/id #uuid "a7717e34-1526-58af-ac56-b4d430bd6476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303998} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf4e45f-a302-583a-bd22-adf4a555d7e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303999} {:answer/id #uuid "addfeb7e-1aec-5fe9-b853-0c3dca698346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304000} {:answer/id #uuid "983e80e8-6dcf-50a5-9c49-dfb064e16956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "addfeb7e-1aec-5fe9-b853-0c3dca698346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304000} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3f05016-fa57-5df8-a6be-3462d426bec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d06c7ee3-551c-5c09-b315-78ac67d71865" :question/math-academy-id "q-339359" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{({e}^{-t})}^{′} \\\\ {(0)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(0)}^{′} \\\\ {({e}^{2t})}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{(t)}^{′} \\\\ {(t+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dfdbdba-247c-5ddd-bb6e-5d9637140264" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Linear System of Differential Equations in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c6592e-4966-56d6-ab38-b37bfe63824f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304004} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0a461a-e5b6-5426-a4c2-d8aeb80e9d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304005} {:answer/id #uuid "9d70fc62-ab5c-507a-a80d-2db3ccdd6c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304006} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3efa34-7715-5049-a9f1-405b1d4757b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304007} {:answer/id #uuid "c5936ffa-924f-5fad-94ed-11a4f798e5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d70fc62-ab5c-507a-a80d-2db3ccdd6c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304006} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac7447a3-d664-5000-be2c-0373b7be3f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40a1d63e-b003-5fa0-a477-198f50d9b5ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-339369" :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dfdbdba-247c-5ddd-bb6e-5d9637140264" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Linear System of Differential Equations in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895459b6-9b29-5222-84f2-a41e0dd8f640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304011} {:answer/id #uuid "571e29bc-6365-59cb-a712-8e760be49fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304012} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1ffcfe-f99c-5d9d-ab05-1a60afa51f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304013} {:answer/id #uuid "96e6d039-387e-51ff-98e3-8dc95a196cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304014} {:answer/id #uuid "130eb812-2442-53a1-b82a-6a4fb3521f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "130eb812-2442-53a1-b82a-6a4fb3521f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304015} :answer-field/id #uuid "2670ea86-b7ad-5b30-bfdd-6ff59ea36087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af37968e-c71b-5339-a737-eeb8f1fda04b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339372" :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)-9{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+5{x}_{1}(t)-9{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+9{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45116c70-2444-55a0-bc12-37757187cb8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304018} {:answer/id #uuid "fef77e2e-4073-59ca-b6f5-a7c1d7f34bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304019} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a736d2-5506-5a3c-a905-c5e23d01cc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304020} {:answer/id #uuid "3aae4226-9b10-5d65-bd85-5cf8df921c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304021} {:answer/id #uuid "062dc9e5-b8af-563f-9962-8633caf3f2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aae4226-9b10-5d65-bd85-5cf8df921c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304021} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ed5985-55dd-5c58-b80d-bbc16f7d5b25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a010ab1-a3f9-5893-b9a2-6fa72f5a0e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-339539" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{7}\\,{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}(t){x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{3}, &  & b(t)=-2, &  & f(t)=1, \\\\ c(t)=\\sqrt{7}, &  & d(t)=5, &  & g(t)=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b765b188-c3b2-57cb-995e-743b4532ae04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304025} {:answer/id #uuid "86cd1c45-8d36-5175-8f1c-c4609467f98a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304026} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b3c01f-7cbd-574f-b490-77ed2fc81410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304027} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce1d150-4cd4-5e2f-991d-db59c0085a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304028} {:answer/id #uuid "600f1dfd-8cfc-5cdc-b7cc-7195365aebdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce1d150-4cd4-5e2f-991d-db59c0085a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304028} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87e08fb-c642-57ea-abf8-72786fa304c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "574f0a63-4469-527b-9813-f3ae276f496a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339540" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t), &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\,t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}), &  & b(t)={13}^{2}, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=9, &  & d(t)=-\\pi , &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157ed49c-3b7f-5b42-99f6-61fa6117f7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304032} {:answer/id #uuid "c263e7a0-1cc0-564d-9274-e98a042dfc4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304033} {:answer/id #uuid "ede834bd-bf1d-53f2-916f-cda550a4dd57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304034} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb31e58-6703-561b-b97a-712ce88e2ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304035} {:answer/id #uuid "34802db2-5541-5cea-9cbd-96e54811501b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bb31e58-6703-561b-b97a-712ce88e2ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304035} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e0a606-eb12-580f-a373-d6f6ccc4ad3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5fa7289-515e-5427-9dab-7c7608567336" :question/math-academy-id "q-339543" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=t\\,{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)=2, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=t, &  & b(t)=-1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the term ${x}_{1}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe0a6f7f-d8c9-51b7-b914-c8bb06f42e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304039} {:answer/id #uuid "53fa6fce-53e7-5957-9227-51eaa1dc57f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304040} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6f7f54-4136-5845-b7be-003178c515bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304041} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed0dbca-1199-5bc5-9e60-5eece5fa65f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304042} {:answer/id #uuid "0755112e-4647-513e-bfa8-66f40554d550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b6f7f54-4136-5845-b7be-003178c515bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304041} :answer-field/id #uuid "64494003-9f2c-5710-af69-e2ed147c07c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "663640a2-ec5f-5fdb-a4f6-3c8d41c9d657" :question/math-academy-id "q-339547" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order linear systems of ODEs?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}^{2}(t)-{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{1}(t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=7, &  & b(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66d19e98-522f-5db2-b340-0f35ba2ece23" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217a4d1e-1bc2-5b3a-9f98-76c6d28bdd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304046} {:answer/id #uuid "c119089c-5120-5ba9-9465-96d1ab95deb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304047} {:answer/id #uuid "0052f7df-558e-58af-849f-2403d1a36883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304048} {:answer/id #uuid "26b666a3-32d8-5b11-acdc-aa7c9ad0b737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304049} {:answer/id #uuid "cf224bf6-6074-5d27-956e-d61e8778fe69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0052f7df-558e-58af-849f-2403d1a36883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #e