099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e16c798-398c-5234-a523-face10349099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304090} :answer-field/id #uuid "8322089d-4d1a-52d0-bb34-10667ad72ffc" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "feec14ee-16b4-5c64-a593-594b58301a5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339648" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system of linear ODEs above can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n| ${x}^{′}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)=t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+t \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a77b0c29-1f31-50cb-8311-695b299ce18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304093} {:answer/id #uuid "51a9627b-e217-5d5e-a4cb-7cd0a4be5df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304094} {:answer/id #uuid "db15d0c5-c9a2-5c35-98bd-4e0513ef3a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304095} {:answer/id #uuid "a909c06c-3a76-5768-b967-087ba7ece2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304096} {:answer/id #uuid "080ce05f-eb4c-557b-8296-052a87ce66a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a77b0c29-1f31-50cb-8311-695b299ce18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304093} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7d6461-8423-5d1e-acec-3f8c0f024d77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "042b9e5d-b5c3-529a-afda-b9159a86ab1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339658" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd877aa-d17a-5b43-8345-fb93ff12594b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304100} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f3b84d-8426-59a7-9f43-f3219b203118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304101} {:answer/id #uuid "78d36dc6-ef40-5827-a856-b8ff754915e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304102} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7602b9-22b3-54c1-bd55-aa3df06290e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304103} {:answer/id #uuid "32107914-144e-51e9-8caa-bcd5d0bbe7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d36dc6-ef40-5827-a856-b8ff754915e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304102} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e11f93-e54b-5044-a5d2-2183e89c189a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a793bc80-4687-5211-9dd0-257312689a0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339661" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "In matrix form, the system\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=A(t)x(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nis *homogeneous* when $f≡0,$ and *inhomogeneous* when $f≢0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since $f≡0.$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since $f=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]≢0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d41b6e0c-7086-5917-a6a1-93aebac40c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304107} {:answer/id #uuid "8d54f8a6-0ee3-5c08-a630-ed1335ebec81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304108} {:answer/id #uuid "6a62a466-0a4f-59ba-9045-259e2310fccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304109} {:answer/id #uuid "69a14d37-7dd6-56cb-bdb8-621990c20473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304110} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5dbe7b-0943-5110-8f5d-f523ba27b89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d54f8a6-0ee3-5c08-a630-ed1335ebec81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304108} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67fdfa8-053c-53b5-a0c3-6f57add7c228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "754a4a00-918b-5836-9ca9-9c07c3873c1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339663" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+t\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-2, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=4, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=0, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfe9c011-f8a8-500d-ba31-317f1cbfc04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304114} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c97f00-d2ba-57a7-b057-5ef34a4d1dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304115} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d21818-1e60-5834-9273-8e554900d4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304116} {:answer/id #uuid "1489a6cc-67aa-5fd8-bd86-b2c3a6c24f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304117} {:answer/id #uuid "a056ae1b-af4e-5bf0-8a7c-26291c697acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1489a6cc-67aa-5fd8-bd86-b2c3a6c24f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304117} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2533cc-915c-5bbd-9f24-9b1df4f32b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64f9647f-5e20-5001-ac5a-8c627a363836" :question/math-academy-id "q-339669" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)+4{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)-4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5){x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-3, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6t) \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-2, &  & g(t)=2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=1, &  & b(t)=3, &  & f(t)=4{e}^{t} \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=-1, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-4, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=1, &  & d(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5), &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28939f65-9fd6-564d-a0c2-73c1038ac898" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0292d3eb-8c30-5b85-a370-59bf5041aaa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304121} {:answer/id #uuid "ac64420b-5fb2-5241-8ada-65c6e9832b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304122} {:answer/id #uuid "9894396e-91e1-5497-af30-a031e9a4f2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304123} {:answer/id #uuid "af1044f4-6752-5d17-9dfd-16d4680ed55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304124} {:answer/id #uuid "6d81802a-e676-57f5-b070-eb325bffddb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d81802a-e676-57f5-b070-eb325bffddb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304125} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf89ee0d-afe7-575b-a18e-4bb55ce45018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0aa1f798-5b62-5ba9-9684-87deffa99c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-339672" :question/problem "Which of the following systems are homogeneous?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+1\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{e}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)+3t\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of the form\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $f≡0$ and $g≡0$ (i.e., both $f$ and $g$ are zero functions), then the system is *homogeneous*. Otherwise, if at least one of $f$ or $g$ is not identically zero, the system is *inhomogeneous*.\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System I is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=3, &  & b(t)=2, &  & f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t) \\\\ c(t)=-1, &  & d(t)=4, &  & g(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System II is homogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≡0$ and $g≡0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=-1, &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0 \\\\ c(t)=5, &  & d(t)=6, &  & g(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System III is inhomogeneous since it can be written in the standard form with $f≢0$ and $g≢0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=4, &  & b(t)=0, &  & f(t)={e}^{2} \\\\ c(t)=0, &  & d(t)=1, &  & g(t)=3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68e5542-cbd0-5e88-9137-8095fda676c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304128} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e4b9a8-1960-52ca-b01a-d16e96e08093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304129} {:answer/id #uuid "be8cb60b-cc94-5d14-9f73-f753f70dd544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130} {:answer/id #uuid "88639ab3-dc58-538a-97c0-27572017b354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304131} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c4201f-ea0c-5013-bd88-4afb6fc05d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be8cb60b-cc94-5d14-9f73-f753f70dd544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130} :answer-field/id #uuid "364a373e-e90d-583c-b598-a5ce5197257b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17775fc6-5357-537a-a61e-e6f980d1d85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339734" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t{e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (t{e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10420dd6-6f61-5db5-912b-c968d4c649c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48875c46-8f23-53f4-b6ca-8a6b43c83a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304135} {:answer/id #uuid "f90897d7-bd3f-5a2f-8fc9-c6245e1455aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304136} {:answer/id #uuid "bfee026b-fd46-5192-aeb2-a167a7cf44bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304137} {:answer/id #uuid "030239e1-4bdd-5ffd-93a2-78777e78cefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{3}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304138} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4e0a2c-27c6-5642-bb91-5c3e266dc689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfee026b-fd46-5192-aeb2-a167a7cf44bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{1}(t)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304137} :answer-field/id #uuid "61893fb5-b971-5a7f-b38b-067e13449e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a5cb12f-fb18-5374-b444-c1c75a3dde15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339744" :question/problem "Consider the matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we need to substitute each vector into the given matrix differential equation and check if it results in a true statement.\n\nFor ${x}_{1}(t)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for ${x}_{2}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for ${x}_{3}(t)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & \\ne [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"${x}_{1}(t)$ only\"."}]}
